    "['step [parameter1]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'sequence 10', 'parent_command': ['ip access-list extended test'], 'root': 'ip access-list extended test', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': '##\\nsequence 10\\n##\\n\\nExplanation: \\n- In Cisco IOS, the equivalent of HUAWEI\\'s `step` command in ACL configuration is `sequence`. \\n- Both serve the same purpose of specifying the order/numbering of ACL rules.\\n- The syntax remains nearly identical, just replacing \"step\" with \"sequence\". \\n\\nNote: This assumes the Cisco device is running IOS that supports sequence numbers (12.4(20)T and later). For older IOS versions, numbered ACLs would use implicit numbering without this command.', 'trans_template': 'sequence 10'}]]": 1,
    "['rule [parameter1] deny protocol [parameter2] source-ip [parameter3] [parameter4]', [{'para_map': [], 'trans_command': 'access-list 10 deny ip host 10.10.1.1 any', 'parent_command': ['ip access-list extended test'], 'root': 'ip access-list extended test', 'source': 'llm', 'origin_reponse': \"Here is the translated Cisco equivalent command for the given Huawei configuration:\\n\\n##\\naccess-list 10 deny ip host 10.10.1.1 any\\n##\\n\\nExplanation:\\n- `rule 10 deny protocol ip source-ip 10.10.1.1 0` in Huawei becomes a standard Cisco ACL entry.\\n- `10` is the ACL number (matching Huawei's rule number in this case).\\n- `deny ip` matches the Huawei `deny protocol ip`.\\n- `host 10.10.1.1` corresponds to the exact source IP (Huawei's `10.10.1.1 0` where mask 0 means exact match).\\n- `any` represents all destinations (implied in Huawei's command when no destination is specified).\", 'trans_template': 'access-list 10 deny ip host 10.10.1.1 any'}]]": 1,
